1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,868
Years ago, while working at Amazon,

2
00:00:06,425 --> 00:00:08,608
I had already reached a conclusion.

3
00:00:09,803 --> 00:00:13,159
A company can afford a lot of desirable jobs without

4
00:00:13,228 --> 00:00:16,363
needing a team of that size to create its business

5
00:00:16,444 --> 00:00:19,893
value. That judgment stayed with me.

6
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:23,654
And as AI takes on more implementation,

7
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:27,833
I think engineers should pay closer attention to where their

8
00:00:27,903 --> 00:00:33,720
work becomes useful. Organizations can grow around management layers,

9
00:00:33,952 --> 00:00:37,261
departmental interests, and internal politics.

10
00:00:37,853 --> 00:00:42,787
Coordination expands. The customer's problem may barely change.

11
00:00:43,716 --> 00:00:48,000
Someone can work very hard while their abilities are absorbed

12
00:00:48,128 --> 00:00:51,089
by work the organization created for itself.

13
00:00:52,389 --> 00:00:58,171
So Amazon's later restructuring and layoffs didn't surprise me.

14
00:00:58,171 --> 00:01:02,745
In twenty twenty-four, Amazon announced plans to reduce layers and

15
00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:07,064
unnecessary processes. The following year,

16
00:01:07,273 --> 00:01:11,406
Andy Jassy said he expected AI efficiency to reduce its

17
00:01:11,476 --> 00:01:14,541
total corporate workforce over time.

18
00:01:15,168 --> 00:01:20,427
Its January twenty twenty-six layoff announcement again emphasized fewer layers

19
00:01:20,497 --> 00:01:26,267
and less bureaucracy. Those statements describe an organization changing.

20
00:01:27,079 --> 00:01:31,422
They cannot tell us what any individual contributed.

21
00:01:31,422 --> 00:01:36,159
My interpretation is that cheaper execution accelerates a reassessment that

22
00:01:36,229 --> 00:01:40,118
was already needed. For the next five to ten years,

23
00:01:40,501 --> 00:01:43,833
I want to invest more in understanding customers,

24
00:01:44,158 --> 00:01:48,907
making product decisions, and carrying worthwhile work into use.

25
00:01:49,290 --> 00:01:53,702
Stronger implementation tools give engineers room to take on more

26
00:01:53,783 --> 00:01:58,833
of that responsibility. Boris Cherny has described having Claude Code

27
00:01:58,868 --> 00:02:01,817
write his code while he continues reviewing it.

28
00:02:02,629 --> 00:02:06,553
Simon Willison describes quickly trying several prototypes.

29
00:02:07,366 --> 00:02:10,501
These are particular practitioners' experiences.

30
00:02:11,802 --> 00:02:15,215
What interests me is how they change our allocation of

31
00:02:15,308 --> 00:02:19,406
time. Once you can try an idea more cheaply,

32
00:02:19,917 --> 00:02:25,339
deciding which idea deserves the effort becomes more valuable.

33
00:02:25,338 --> 00:02:28,647
The first place I'd invest is a business domain.

34
00:02:29,239 --> 00:02:31,108
Learn who uses a product,

35
00:02:31,689 --> 00:02:36,054
who pays, and who bears the consequences when it fails.

36
00:02:37,308 --> 00:02:41,662
Kent Beck used payroll to illustrate how much responsibility sits

37
00:02:41,731 --> 00:02:44,471
behind apparently simple functionality.

38
00:02:45,423 --> 00:02:48,488
Calculating an amount is only part of the work.

39
00:02:49,429 --> 00:02:53,307
Keep learning those obligations through actual projects.

40
00:02:54,259 --> 00:02:58,763
Over time, you'll notice needs that haven't reached a requirements

41
00:02:58,833 --> 00:03:02,722
document. Then get closer to customers.

42
00:03:03,895 --> 00:03:07,122
Imagine a tool that processes business documents.

43
00:03:07,517 --> 00:03:09,444
This is a hypothetical example.

44
00:03:10,304 --> 00:03:13,241
The demonstration extracts every field beautifully.

45
00:03:14,239 --> 00:03:16,155
But when you watch someone work,

46
00:03:16,782 --> 00:03:18,616
you discover where they pause:

47
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:22,970
an unusual document, a missing confirmation,

48
00:03:23,574 --> 00:03:25,397
a result they don't trust.

49
00:03:26,511 --> 00:03:29,855
That pause can tell you more about the next feature

50
00:03:30,029 --> 00:03:34,209
than another hour polishing the demo.

51
00:03:34,209 --> 00:03:36,601
Ask the person to show you how they handle that

52
00:03:36,647 --> 00:03:39,050
case today. Who checks it?

53
00:03:39,654 --> 00:03:41,384
How do they correct a mistake?

54
00:03:41,988 --> 00:03:45,180
What must continue working in their existing system?

55
00:03:45,993 --> 00:03:50,173
You may discover that exception handling deserves attention first.

56
00:03:50,684 --> 00:03:53,517
Use some of the time AI saves to have that

57
00:03:53,575 --> 00:03:58,590
conversation, and come back after delivery to see what changed.

58
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:01,272
The third investment is product judgment.

59
00:04:02,282 --> 00:04:05,777
Willison says he often tries three design approaches.

60
00:04:06,647 --> 00:04:10,072
He also advocates watching real people use the software.

61
00:04:10,931 --> 00:04:13,056
For our imagined document tool,

62
00:04:13,195 --> 00:04:16,585
compare two approaches: verify every item,

63
00:04:17,096 --> 00:04:19,151
or highlight only the exceptions.

64
00:04:19,662 --> 00:04:21,682
Build enough to answer the question.

65
00:04:22,193 --> 00:04:25,386
Then watch where a user hesitates and whether they can

66
00:04:25,467 --> 00:04:27,754
finish the original task.

67
00:04:27,754 --> 00:04:31,005
Before building, write down what you need to learn.

68
00:04:32,177 --> 00:04:35,544
Afterward, ask whether the attempt answered it.

69
00:04:36,276 --> 00:04:39,074
This is how product judgment develops:

70
00:04:39,712 --> 00:04:41,988
choosing what belongs, what can wait,

71
00:04:42,719 --> 00:04:46,075
and which complexity the business actually requires.

72
00:04:46,771 --> 00:04:48,908
AI can propose alternatives.

73
00:04:49,418 --> 00:04:52,518
Your contact with the work gives you a basis for

74
00:04:52,576 --> 00:04:57,243
choosing among them. The fourth investment is commercial judgment.

75
00:04:57,905 --> 00:05:01,318
A useful product still asks someone to change.

76
00:05:02,096 --> 00:05:03,571
Money is one cost.

77
00:05:03,884 --> 00:05:08,075
Migration, learning, integration, and trust matter too.

78
00:05:08,853 --> 00:05:12,464
Understand why the customer's existing approach has survived.

79
00:05:13,765 --> 00:05:17,492
Then test whether your improvement is worth that disruption.

80
00:05:18,792 --> 00:05:22,600
A working prototype gives you something concrete to discuss with

81
00:05:22,658 --> 00:05:27,023
a potential customer. Would they spend time trying it?

82
00:05:27,441 --> 00:05:29,403
Would they discuss an integration?

83
00:05:29,833 --> 00:05:31,261
Who controls the budget,

84
00:05:31,574 --> 00:05:33,966
and where do they look for alternatives?

85
00:05:35,034 --> 00:05:38,076
Include model usage, maintenance,

86
00:05:38,227 --> 00:05:40,166
and support in your estimate.

87
00:05:41,222 --> 00:05:46,064
Cheaper development gives some previously expensive ideas another chance.

88
00:05:47,364 --> 00:05:50,417
Contact with the market tells you which ones deserve to

89
00:05:50,499 --> 00:05:55,247
continue. Finally, take a project through a complete cycle.

90
00:05:56,268 --> 00:06:02,236
Microsoft Digital reported that faster individual development initially failed to

91
00:06:02,317 --> 00:06:08,099
improve team productivity. It later brought business intent and acceptance

92
00:06:08,146 --> 00:06:11,094
criteria into a shared specification.

93
00:06:11,907 --> 00:06:14,171
That's an organizational account,

94
00:06:14,496 --> 00:06:16,923
but it highlights something practical:

95
00:06:17,735 --> 00:06:21,810
progress depends on the connections across the work.

96
00:06:21,810 --> 00:06:23,157
Agree on the goal.

97
00:06:23,517 --> 00:06:27,476
Connect the systems. Check real failure cases.

98
00:06:28,137 --> 00:06:29,740
Help the user get started,

99
00:06:29,774 --> 00:06:32,015
and return to learn what happened.

100
00:06:32,793 --> 00:06:36,589
I recently used AI on a reporting task across several

101
00:06:36,659 --> 00:06:39,863
systems and verified it in development.

102
00:06:41,164 --> 00:06:44,565
It made me more willing to take on complete tasks.

103
00:06:45,866 --> 00:06:51,810
Engineering experience helps make the result something others can trust.

104
00:06:51,811 --> 00:06:55,468
The judgment I reached at Amazon still matters to me.

105
00:06:56,049 --> 00:06:59,021
A role can be provided by an organization.

106
00:06:59,926 --> 00:07:03,792
My abilities need an ongoing connection to real needs.

107
00:07:04,454 --> 00:07:07,938
Each project is a chance to strengthen that connection:

108
00:07:08,599 --> 00:07:10,724
understand a customer more deeply,

109
00:07:11,328 --> 00:07:13,243
make a better product choice,

110
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:16,715
and learn what it takes for someone to adopt the

111
00:07:16,784 --> 00:07:20,789
result. If AI can handle much more work in five

112
00:07:20,858 --> 00:07:24,991
or ten years, I want to understand more about what

113
00:07:25,038 --> 00:07:26,373
people need by then.

114
00:07:27,476 --> 00:07:31,110
That growth can begin with the next conversation and the

115
00:07:31,191 --> 00:07:37,043
next project. Stronger tools make me optimistic about what engineers

116
00:07:37,124 --> 00:07:41,025
can attempt. I want to use that opportunity to make

117
00:07:41,071 --> 00:07:44,659
things I care about that someone else actually needs.
